1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,865
A royal treasurer rides home in his

2
00:00:02,865 --> 00:00:05,031
chariot, reading a scroll out loud.

3
00:00:05,481 --> 00:00:08,787
He serves a queen whose people
once made Rome back down.

4
00:00:09,237 --> 00:00:11,931
A stranger runs up beside
the chariot and asks:

5
00:00:11,931 --> 00:00:14,165
do you understand what you are reading?

6
00:00:14,615 --> 00:00:18,455
His answer may be the most honest
line in Acts. What did he say?

7
00:00:20,405 --> 00:00:24,014
Acts calls him a man of
Ethiopia, of great authority

8
00:00:24,014 --> 00:00:26,720
under Candace, queen of the Ethiopians.

9
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,873
But Candace was not her
name. Kandake was a

10
00:00:29,873 --> 00:00:31,885
title: the queen mother of Kush.

11
00:00:32,335 --> 00:00:35,724
The Bible's Ethiopia here
is Kush, on the Nile, with

12
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:38,265
its capital at Meroë, in today's Sudan.

13
00:00:38,715 --> 00:00:42,632
The Greek writer Strabo describes
a Kandake of his own day:

14
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:45,819
a masculine sort of woman,
and blind in one eye.

15
00:00:46,269 --> 00:00:49,524
Her army pulled down statues
of Caesar. Her envoys

16
00:00:49,524 --> 00:00:52,648
reached Augustus, and got
everything they asked.

17
00:00:53,098 --> 00:00:55,807
So what was her treasurer
reading on the Gaza road?

18
00:00:57,757 --> 00:01:01,101
He had gone up to Jerusalem
to worship. Now he sat in

19
00:01:01,101 --> 00:01:03,624
his chariot, reading the prophet Isaiah.

20
00:01:04,074 --> 00:01:06,741
He read aloud. That is
how Philip heard him.

21
00:01:07,191 --> 00:01:09,196
He was led as a sheep to the slaughter.

22
00:01:09,646 --> 00:01:12,889
Philip asks: Understandest
thou what thou readest?

23
00:01:13,339 --> 00:01:17,563
And the treasurer answers: How can
I, except some man should guide me?

24
00:01:18,013 --> 00:01:21,080
A powerful man, asking
a stranger on foot for

25
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:23,602
help. Then he asks the real question.

26
00:01:25,552 --> 00:01:29,776
Of whom speaketh the prophet this?
Of himself, or of some other man?

27
00:01:30,226 --> 00:01:32,999
Philip climbs up beside
him and, beginning at

28
00:01:32,999 --> 00:01:35,218
that same scripture, preaches Jesus.

29
00:01:35,668 --> 00:01:38,537
The road passes water.
See, here is water, he

30
00:01:38,537 --> 00:01:41,151
says. What doth hinder me to be baptized?

31
00:01:41,601 --> 00:01:43,969
And he commands the
chariot to stand still.

32
00:01:45,919 --> 00:01:49,951
They go down into the water, both
of them, and Philip baptizes him.

33
00:01:50,401 --> 00:01:52,805
Then the Spirit of the Lord catches Philip

34
00:01:52,805 --> 00:01:54,923
away. The treasurer sees him no more.

35
00:01:55,373 --> 00:01:57,315
And he went on his way rejoicing.

36
00:01:57,765 --> 00:01:59,706
Acts never says where the water was.

37
00:02:00,156 --> 00:02:03,292
At Ein Hanniya, near
Jerusalem, archaeologists

38
00:02:03,292 --> 00:02:06,769
uncovered a Byzantine pool
at the foot of a church.

39
00:02:07,219 --> 00:02:10,099
Some early Christians
pointed to this spring.

40
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:13,107
Ethiopian Christians still
hold services there.

41
00:02:13,557 --> 00:02:15,541
But where did he go, rejoicing?

42
00:02:17,491 --> 00:02:20,564
Did you know? Look up
Acts 8:37 in a modern

43
00:02:20,564 --> 00:02:23,422
Bible, and you may find only a footnote.

44
00:02:23,872 --> 00:02:27,042
In the King James, the
treasurer says: I believe

45
00:02:27,042 --> 00:02:29,419
that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.

46
00:02:29,869 --> 00:02:33,523
The oldest Greek copies of Acts
do not have that sentence.

47
00:02:33,523 --> 00:02:36,674
Erasmus printed it, and it
reached the King James.

48
00:02:37,124 --> 00:02:39,678
The faith in it is the Church's faith. The

49
00:02:39,678 --> 00:02:42,415
footnote is only about
which copies carry it.

50
00:02:44,365 --> 00:02:48,781
Acts never gives his name. Ethiopian
tradition calls him Bachos.

51
00:02:49,231 --> 00:02:53,326
Around the year 180, Irenaeus
wrote that he was sent into the

52
00:02:53,326 --> 00:02:56,548
regions of Ethiopia, to
preach what he believed.

53
00:02:56,998 --> 00:03:00,452
Eusebius, about 325, calls
him the first‑fruits

54
00:03:00,452 --> 00:03:02,950
of believers throughout the world.

55
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,841
Even Eusebius is careful. He
is said, he writes, to have

56
00:03:06,841 --> 00:03:09,544
been the first to preach
in his own country.

57
00:03:11,494 --> 00:03:14,971
He was not the Bible's first
Ethiopian at a royal court.

58
00:03:15,421 --> 00:03:17,811
Six centuries earlier, Ebed‑melech the

59
00:03:17,811 --> 00:03:20,264
Ethiopian pulled Jeremiah out of a pit.

60
00:03:20,714 --> 00:03:23,055
And the psalm says: Ethiopia shall soon

61
00:03:23,055 --> 00:03:24,917
stretch out her hands unto God.

62
00:03:25,367 --> 00:03:28,176
At Aksum, a king turned
to Christ in the three

63
00:03:28,176 --> 00:03:31,169
hundreds, with Frumentius.
That is another story.

64
00:03:33,119 --> 00:03:36,938
Notice what Acts leaves out:
no name, no place, no ending.

65
00:03:37,388 --> 00:03:39,793
Later writers filled the gaps. Some are

66
00:03:39,793 --> 00:03:41,889
early witnesses. Some are guesses.

67
00:03:42,339 --> 00:03:45,155
So when a post claims
to know exactly who he

68
00:03:45,155 --> 00:03:47,331
was, ask: which book, and how old?

69
00:03:49,281 --> 00:03:51,329
The treasurer had power, money and a

70
00:03:51,329 --> 00:03:53,377
scroll. What he lacked, he admitted.

71
00:03:53,827 --> 00:03:57,376
We swing between pride that
reads alone and trusts every hot

72
00:03:57,376 --> 00:04:00,334
take, and despair that says
scripture is too hard.

73
00:04:00,784 --> 00:04:04,273
The quiet path is his: read
slowly, ask the Church to

74
00:04:04,273 --> 00:04:06,907
guide you, and go on your way rejoicing.

75
00:04:07,357 --> 00:04:11,346
Maybe tonight, read Acts 8
slowly, out loud, the way he did.

76
00:04:13,296 --> 00:04:15,387
Thank you for sitting with us over coffee.

77
00:04:15,837 --> 00:04:17,309
This is Ethiopics.
